Magna Carta, 1215:-
Established that no one is above the law. Stated that the law should be impartial, and no free man should be convicted without a fair trial

Bill of Rights, 1689:-
William III agreed to govern only with the consent of Parliament, establishing the UK as a constitutional monarchy

Entick Vs Carrington, 1765:-
Lord Camden laid down the principle that the government can only act according to the law protecting the rights of citizens from despotic rule

Somerset VS Stewart, 1772:-
Lord Maselfield ruled that slavery was illegal in the UK since it had not been legislated for by an act of Parliament and was unsupported by Common law

Represenation of the people act, 1928:-
Established the principle of universal franchise in the UK

1950: European Convention on Human Rights:-
Members of the Council of Europe used the 1948 Universal Declaration of Human Rights to draw up this treaty to secure basic rights both for their own citizens and for other nationalities with their borders. The Convention was signed in Rome in 1950, ratified by the UK in 1951 and came into force in 1953 - but was not officially part of UK law. Unlike the Universal Declaration, the European Convention on Human Rights contains rights which can be relied on in a court of law

1966: UK signs up to the European Court of Human Rights:-
Six years after the European Court of Human Rights was created, the UK granted what is known as ‘individual petition’ - the right for people to their cases directly to the court in Strasbourg.

AFTER 1997:-
In 1997, Tony Blair’s Labour government came to power and ushered in a rights-based culture that hugely accelerated progress in this area, chiefly through three pieces of legislation:

Human Rights Act (HRA), 1998:-
The 1950 European Convention on Human Rights was finally incorporated into British law, entering into force in 2000. Previously, UK citizens relied on ‘negative rights’, i.e. people were allowed to do anything that was not expressly forbidden by law, and which might be covered in a patchwork of different statue and common law rights, but these rights were harder to protect; the HRA at last replaced these with ‘positive rights’ - a clear statement of their human rights, enforceable in the UK law courts and politically (if not legally) binding on Parliament

Freedom of Information Act, 2000:-
UK citizens could now ask to see any information held about them by public bodies (e..g. tax authorities, government departments, schools), as well as information held by these bodies that be of general interest to the public. Individuals and the media can now more easily hold the government to account (e.g. the MPs expenses scandal, 2009).

Equality Act, 2010:-
Previously, the 1965 Race Relations Act (protecting against racial discrimination) and the 1970 Equal Pay Act (preventing lower pay for women doing the same jobs as men), failed to establish equality more broadly as they left out other important groups (disability, LGBT+, etc). The 2010 Equality Act rectified this, bring together over 116 separate pieces of legislation, applying to any kind of group, and preventing any kind of discrimination but in particular when it comes to employment, pay, government services, healthcare, housing, education, financial services, policing and law enforcement
